The keyword of bacru is "utter", because "orgle-borgle", "squeak", and othe=
r
such onomatopeia are all utterances. A creaky door is a valid {lo bacru},
but it is not a valid {lo cusku}. I think the salient difference is that {l=
o
se cusku} is something imparting meaning, whereas {lo se bacru} is merely
some sort of noise.

I do agree with Robin that "express" is a better keyword than "say", not
because "say" doesn't work, but because it is misleading. Someone learning
that {cusku} means "x1 says x2 ...." will very probably assume that this
means *only* verbal expression of a concept, which is not true of {cusku}.

However, {bacru} is definitely not "say". If we want to fiddle with
semantics, the appropriate lojbo valsi (IMO) for "say" would be {ba'usku}
(bacru cusku): "x1 says (verbally expresses) x2 to x3 via medium x4"

2010/3/12 Jorge Llamb=EDas <jjllambias@gmail.com>

